<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Web on 0x32++</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/web/</link><description>Recent content in Web on 0x32++</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Fri, 21 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0100</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://blog.0x32.co.uk/tags/web/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Doomheaven Moves to GitHub Pages</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/doomheaven-github-pages/</link><pubDate>Fri, 21 Aug 2026 08:00:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/doomheaven-github-pages/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://doomheaven.uk"&gt;doomheaven.uk&lt;/a&gt; is moving house.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;It started life as a Doom fan site back in the late 90s — WADs, screenshots, whatever bits of Doom nonsense felt worth putting on the web at the time. It&amp;rsquo;s sat online quietly ever since, hosted the whole way through by &lt;a href="https://teamhellspawn.com"&gt;teamhellspawn.com&lt;/a&gt;, who&amp;rsquo;ve now let me know they&amp;rsquo;re winding things down.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;I want to say a proper thank you to them for that. Free webspace for the better part of three decades is a genuinely long time to keep something ticking over for no reason beyond being decent about it, and rather than just switching it off, they gave me the chance to get everything off in one piece before it went. Not everyone bothers with that on the way out, so it was appreciated.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Spider Web</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/spider-web/</link><pubDate>Sat, 19 Oct 2013 09:37:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/spider-web/</guid><description>&lt;div xmlns='http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml'&gt;&lt;img style='max-width:586px;' src='http://farm6.staticflickr.com/5501/10358769754_bf344c7301_b.jpg'/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div&gt;http://flic.kr/p/gMnpmw, October 19, 2013 at 10:40AM&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>Day 11, Web based</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/day-11-web-based/</link><pubDate>Mon, 08 Apr 2013 08:22:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/day-11-web-based/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; the only link in this post is now broken. Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;div xmlns='http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml'&gt;&lt;img style='max-width:586px;' src='http://farm9.staticflickr.com/8121/8631152606_8a9134ea3e_b.jpg'/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div&gt;Web based email client, not happy. http://flic.kr/p/e9GUXY, April 08, 2013 at 10:10AM&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>Spider Frost</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/spider-frost/</link><pubDate>Sun, 20 Jan 2013 16:42:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/spider-frost/</guid><description>&lt;div xmlns='http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml'&gt;&lt;img style='max-width:586px;' src='http://farm9.staticflickr.com/8364/8399079938_878e3f6d19_b.jpg'/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div&gt;http://flic.kr/p/dNctR5, January 17, 2013 at 12:32PM&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>icy spider web</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/icy-spider-web/</link><pubDate>Thu, 17 Jan 2013 13:08:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/icy-spider-web/</guid><description>&lt;div xmlns='http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml'&gt;&lt;img style='max-width:586px;' src='http://farm9.staticflickr.com/8377/8388598863_75e40afb39_b.jpg'/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div&gt;Taken on dog walk http://flic.kr/p/dMgLbT, January 17, 2013 at 12:58PM&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>Twitter on Lynx</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/twitter-on-lynx/</link><pubDate>Mon, 14 Sep 2009 09:10:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/twitter-on-lynx/</guid><description>&lt;div style="float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3918331357/" title="photo sharing"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm4.static.flickr.com/3495/3918331357_ab43f399d4_m.jpg" alt="" style="border: solid 2px #000000;" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-top: 0px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3918331357/"&gt;Twitter on Lynx&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Originally uploaded by &lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/people/doodle_m/"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Been trying to use Twitter on Lynx browser, the mobile version of Twitter seems to work okay but can't login using the regular version.&lt;br clear="all" /&gt;</description></item><item><title>Opera 10 Release Candidate</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-10-release-candidate/</link><pubDate>Wed, 26 Aug 2009 12:00:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/opera-10-release-candidate/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a onblur="try {parent.deselectBloggerImageGracefully();} catch(e) {}" href="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjeZ-Vn1QB6GZBwuKALE28V1dz4hu1bxwZONicigVByN7BuL7OP6VL8T11wtDVV5uAJ7RQjCcLsD-O7VB8Dz1UCc1z_Zalu8R-YEU-GXOo-rXgTABgCpCyeQy8AXhbE3GdW4TbMFfDo4BA/s1600-h/opera10rc.png"&gt;&lt;img style="cursor:pointer; cursor:hand;width: 320px; height: 230px;" src="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjeZ-Vn1QB6GZBwuKALE28V1dz4hu1bxwZONicigVByN7BuL7OP6VL8T11wtDVV5uAJ7RQjCcLsD-O7VB8Dz1UCc1z_Zalu8R-YEU-GXOo-rXgTABgCpCyeQy8AXhbE3GdW4TbMFfDo4BA/s320/opera10rc.png" border="0" alt="" id="BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5374200115546137986" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Just installed the &lt;a href="http://www.opera.com/browser/next/"&gt;Opera 10 Release Candidate&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;I have been using the beta versions since they came out and it has become my browser of choice at work and at home.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://meekgonkey.blogspot.com/"&gt;Doodle's Geek Monkey&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a href="http://twitter.com/alastair_hm"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;</description></item><item><title>URL Shortening Rant</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/url-shortening-rant/</link><pubDate>Tue, 25 Aug 2009 08:03:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/url-shortening-rant/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a onblur="try {parent.deselectBloggerImageGracefully();} catch(e) {}" href="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEi_-OsPjk4x4nbd-fY7HMAKfvWVgRbN34lEiqVqczT9KL9dz3e-VRRr_psY4JVmjwt0pyc6I00pIm0sI6Evw-2AwLMXEoeK1W_JNtwKhUO8_OxBioMRt9yIwJY3M533mRRDMnSKGrtKJr8/s1600-h/trim.png"&gt;&lt;img style="cursor:pointer; cursor:hand;width: 320px; height: 160px;" src="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEi_-OsPjk4x4nbd-fY7HMAKfvWVgRbN34lEiqVqczT9KL9dz3e-VRRr_psY4JVmjwt0pyc6I00pIm0sI6Evw-2AwLMXEoeK1W_JNtwKhUO8_OxBioMRt9yIwJY3M533mRRDMnSKGrtKJr8/s320/trim.png" border="0" alt="" id="BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5373794084798826850" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;p&gt;URL shortening is a great idea for Twitter&amp;rsquo;s 120 character limit but it does have its problems. Most annoying, you find an interesting link and click on it and get presented with the screen above. There is nothing you can do to get to the article you clicked on until the server load goes down.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Other problems include you don&amp;rsquo;t know what site you will end up on so can end up on a phishing site or something NSFW, although TinyURLs preview option kind of helps that problem.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Google Chrome on Mint Linux</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/google-chrome-on-mint-linux/</link><pubDate>Tue, 07 Jul 2009 11:51:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/google-chrome-on-mint-linux/</guid><description>&lt;div style="float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745906/" title="photo sharing"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm3.static.flickr.com/2605/3697745906_ed5976d1e2_m.jpg" alt="" style="border: solid 2px #000000;" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style=" margin-top: 0px;font-size:0.9em;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745906/"&gt;Google Chrome on Mint Linux&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Originally uploaded by &lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/people/doodle_m/"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;Testing the Dev build of Chrome running on my Mint Linux VM.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Feels pretty nippy, especially scrolling through Google Reader. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3697745990/" title="Google Chrome on Mint Linux by Alastair Montgomery, on Flickr"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm3.static.flickr.com/2498/3697745990_9387b939fb.jpg" width="500" height="345" alt="Google Chrome on Mint Linux" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;</description></item><item><title>Nibbo Search</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/nibbo-search/</link><pubDate>Fri, 03 Jul 2009 10:30:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/nibbo-search/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 1 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 4 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;div style="float: right; margin-left: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3683401401/" title="photo sharing"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm4.static.flickr.com/3629/3683401401_78207e5ee3_m.jpg" alt="" style="border: solid 2px #000000;" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;span style=" margin-top: 0px;font-size:0.9em;"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3683401401/"&gt;Nibbo Search&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Originally uploaded by &lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/people/doodle_m/"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Been testing a new search engine &lt;a href="http://www.nibbo.com/"&gt;http://www.nibbo.com/&lt;/a&gt; so far I like it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Its really a nice front end on existing search engines so you can search a number of things at the same time.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Geek Chart</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/geek-chart/</link><pubDate>Thu, 28 May 2009 07:00:00 +0100</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/geek-chart/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;A Geek Chart is a web badge that shows where you share stuff online.&lt;br /&gt;Each slice of the Geek Chart is a link to your profile on sites like Flickr, Twitter, Youtube and more.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Here is my chart.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;object width="200" height="200"&gt;&lt;param name="movie" value="http://www.geekchart.com/img/geekchart.swf?username=alastair_hm"&gt;&lt;param name="wmode" value="transparent"&gt;&lt;embed src="http://www.geekchart.com/img/geekchart.swf?username=alastair_hm" wmode="transparent" type="application/x-shockwave-flash" allowscriptaccess="always" width="200" height="200"&gt;&lt;/embed&gt;&lt;/object&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.geekchart.com/user/alastair_hm"&gt;Alastair_hm&amp;rsquo;s Geek Chart&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://meekgonkey.blogspot.com/"&gt;Doodle&amp;rsquo;s Geek Monkey&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a href="http://twitter.com/alastair_hm"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Many Feeds to Feed</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/many-feeds-to-feed/</link><pubDate>Wed, 11 Mar 2009 10:17:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/many-feeds-to-feed/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3345791177/" title="Many feeds to feed by Alastair Montgomery, on Flickr"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm4.static.flickr.com/3659/3345791177_43cb519a9b.jpg" width="500" height="391" alt="Many feeds to feed" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;I sat down and mapped out how all my internet feeds map together.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;The main gathering point is my Twitter account. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;I also seem to have a Twitter addiction which can be seen from this graph&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.flickr.com/photos/doodle_m/3347126002/" title="Twitter Addiction by Alastair Montgomery, on Flickr"&gt;&lt;img src="http://farm4.static.flickr.com/3560/3347126002_1c8658a675_o.jpg" width="630" height="590" alt="Twitter Addiction" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;November 2008 looks like when I discovered &lt;a href="http://twitterfeed.com/"&gt;TwitterFeed&lt;/a&gt; to like my other post through to my Twitter account.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://meekgonkey.blogspot.com/"&gt;Doodle&amp;rsquo;s Geek Monkey&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a href="http://twitter.com/alastair_hm"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>My first wxPython application</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/my-first-wxpython-application/</link><pubDate>Fri, 05 Dec 2008 16:30:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/my-first-wxpython-application/</guid><description>&lt;div&gt;Here's a screenshot of my first wxPython application.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;a onblur="try {parent.deselectBloggerImageGracefully();} catch(e) {}" href="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEj219aUo2kptLNn-KAguks2R-kI3iXgfSr97kHp9NVLk0D37qSJOqx1KkfR1YWAr1tKRvd1yGyHO06TNAiEYv4UQNLWpUlLG5VN8_2xgSdwaIoSzVNtLk7RmvVtYl7d9heo5cPBVTz01Yk/s1600-h/ScreenShot006.jpg"&gt;&lt;img style="cursor:pointer; cursor:hand;width: 379px; height: 400px;" src="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEj219aUo2kptLNn-KAguks2R-kI3iXgfSr97kHp9NVLk0D37qSJOqx1KkfR1YWAr1tKRvd1yGyHO06TNAiEYv4UQNLWpUlLG5VN8_2xgSdwaIoSzVNtLk7RmvVtYl7d9heo5cPBVTz01Yk/s400/ScreenShot006.jpg" border="0" alt="" id="BLOGGER_PHOTO_ID_5276321081649795330" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;div&gt;It grabs the recently listened to tracks from a Last.FM RSS feed and creates a summary which can then be posted to Twitter.&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;I'm well chuffed considering I hadn't written anything in Python at the start of this week.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="http://meekgonkey.blogspot.com/"&gt;Doodle's Geek Monkey&lt;/a&gt; by &lt;a href="http://twitter.com/alastair_hm"&gt;Alastair Montgomery&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description></item><item><title>60 Really Cool and Creative Error 404 Pages</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-60-really-cool-and-creative/</link><pubDate>Fri, 05 Dec 2008 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-60-really-cool-and-creative/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 113 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 135 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Some cool 404 pages :) &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base18719866"&gt; &lt;table id="table18719866" width="100%" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="5" bg="" style="color:#bde0ed;"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.hongkiat.com/blog"&gt;&lt;b&gt;hongkiat.com&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Online Tips For Tech Users, Bloggers and Designers &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items18719866" style="display:block; position:block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: hongkiat.com" href="http://feedproxy.google.com/~r/24thfloor/~3/XkIhOD2XFqE/"&gt;60 Really Cool and Creative Error 404 Pages&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; By hongkiat &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;!--INFOLINKS_OFF--&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Having a good Error 404 page is perhaps as important as having great contents. Sometimes it may not be your fault visitors landed on error pages, but being able to communicate and direct them back to the home page is as good as a second chance to re-engage a visitor. &lt;em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Error 404 page helps you does that.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Melt Mail is Another Quick, Disposable Email Service</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/melt-mail-is-another-quick-disposable/</link><pubDate>Wed, 26 Nov 2008 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/melt-mail-is-another-quick-disposable/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 23 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 25 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;p&gt;I&amp;rsquo;m melting, melting&amp;hellip;&amp;hellip;&amp;hellip; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table9477167" bg cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0" width="100%" style="color:#bde0ed;"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt; &lt;td rowspan="2"&gt; &lt;table cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;span class="blogtitle"&gt;&lt;a href="http://pipes.yahoo.com/pipes/pipe.info?_id=8IsQFi282xG5FaTJJjBjOg"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Life GH Com&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;br /&gt;Pipes Output &lt;/td&gt; &lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt; &lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;/tbody&gt;&lt;/table&gt;&lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt; &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;div id="items9477167" style="display: block;"&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr bgcolor="#ffffff"&gt; &lt;td&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;h2&gt;&lt;a title="Site: Life GH Com" href="http://feeds.gawker.com/%7Er/lifehacker/full/%7E3/sDwz-jpGTd0/melt-mail-is-another-quick-disposable-email-service"&gt;Melt Mail is Another Quick, Disposable Email Service [Email]&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="http://cache.gawker.com/assets/images/lifehacker/2008/11/melt-mail.png" height="297" width="305" /&gt;Melt Mail is a free email forwarding service that creates a temporary email address that forwards all incoming mail to your real email address. To use it, just enter the email address you want messages forwarded to, select how long you want it to work, then hit Create. Melt Mail generates a random address that you can use with impunity to register on spam-likely sites, for example, and after 3, 6, 12, or 24 hours, the forwarding will stop. We&amp;rsquo;ve &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/email-forwarding/get-a-temporary-email-address-with-spambox-199943.php"&gt;covered&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/disposable-email-address/get-a-15+minute-email-address-from-guerrillamail-305933.php"&gt;boat&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/disposable-email-address/disposable-email-addresses-with-10-minute-mail-287945.php"&gt;loads&lt;/a&gt; of &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/email/get-a-temporary-email-address-129052.php"&gt;similar&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/notag/pookmail-disposable-email-addresses-38794.php"&gt;disposable&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/spam-filters/mailinator-public-spamfree-email-inbox-030870.php"&gt;email&lt;/a&gt; &lt;a rel="nofollow" target="_blank" href="http://lifehacker.com/software/disposable-email-address/avoid-spam-by-registering-with-2-prong-214977.php"&gt;applications&lt;/a&gt;, so Melt Mail isn&amp;rsquo;t breaking any new ground, but since some services won&amp;rsquo;t allow you to register with popular temporary email addresses, it&amp;rsquo;s worthwhile to have another option in your arsenal. Besides, the dead-simple interface is a nice touch. &lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Lunascape Is Firefox, Chrome, and Internet Explorer Rolled Into One [Featured Windows Early Adopter Download]</title><link>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-lunascape-is-firefox-chrome/</link><pubDate>Tue, 25 Nov 2008 12:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://blog.0x32.co.uk/posts/bloglines-lunascape-is-firefox-chrome/</guid><description>&lt;blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Note:&lt;/strong&gt; 19 of this post&amp;rsquo;s 20 links are now broken — mostly dead 2008–2013 feed-syndication infrastructure (FeedBurner, Gawker, AOL&amp;rsquo;s old blog network, Pheedo, Yahoo Pipes). Left as originally published.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="http://www.bloglines.com/"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Going on my download list. &lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;hr /&gt; &lt;table width="100%"&gt; &lt;tbody&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt; &lt;a name="base9477167"&gt; &lt;/a&gt;&lt;table id="table9477167" bg="" style="color: rg